 { Step 1 Cycle 1: Solve as many exercises as you can manage in four weeks. These exercises are your set; and solving them brings you to the end of your first cycle. The four-week time period can be adjusted according to your lifestyle and circumstances, but try not to spend much more than four weeks. If you find yourself taking significantly longer than four weeks, you have probably either not been putting in sufficient time, or have included too many exercises in your set. Step 2 Take a break from chess for at least a clear day, and up to a week if you need it. Step 3 Cycle 2: Solve the same set of exercises but faster: within two weeks is the target. Step 4 Repeat steps 2 and 3, and repeat again. Aim to complete each cycle in half the number of days as the previous cycle (rounded up, when dealing with an odd number of days) . Step 5 The Woodpecker Method has been completed when the full set of exercises has been solved entirely in one day – or after the 7th cycle, if you are unable to solve the full set in a day. In the final two cycles, you should focus more on spotting ideas, patterns and motifs at speed, and less on the finer details of calculation. }  
